1. Database Operations
CREATE DATABASE
CREATE DATABASE Company;
ALTER DATABASE (rename) — SQL Server
ALTER DATABASE Test1 MODIFY NAME = 'Company';
USE (select the database to work in)
USE Company;
💡 USE must run before creating tables — otherwise they land in the wrong database.

2. Table Operations
CREATE TABLE
CREATE TABLE Employees (
    EmployeeID   INT           PRIMARY KEY NOT NULL,
    EmployeeName VARCHAR(50)   NOT NULL,
    Phone        INT           NOT NULL,
    DepId        INT           NOT NULL
);
ALTER TABLE (add a constraint later)
ALTER TABLE Employees
ADD CONSTRAINT U_phone UNIQUE (Phone);
Rename a table — SQL Server
EXEC sp_rename 'tableEmployees', 'Employee';
DROP TABLE (deletes the table AND its data — irreversible)
DROP TABLE Employees;
3. Data Types (SQL Server)
Data Type	From	To	Category
INT	-2,147,483,648	2,147,483,647	Number
BIT	0 (False/No/Off)	1 (True/Yes/On)	Number
DECIMAL	-10^38 + 1	10^38 - 1	Number
DATETIME	Jan 1, 1753	Dec 31, 9999	Date
CHAR	Fixed length	Max 8,000 characters (fixed-length, non-Unicode)	String
VARCHAR	Variable length	Max 8,000 characters (variable-length, non-Unicode)	String
BINARY	—	Max 8,000 bytes (fixed-length binary data)	Binary
CHAR vs VARCHAR — the classic interview trap
CHAR(10)	VARCHAR(10)
Storage	Always uses 10 chars	Uses only what's needed
'AB' stored as	'AB ' (padded)	'AB'
Best for	Fixed values (country codes)	Names, emails, addresses
4. Constraints
Purpose: accuracy and reliability of data in the database.

4.1 NOT NULL — column cannot hold NULL
CREATE TABLE Employees (
    EmployeeID   INT         PRIMARY KEY NOT NULL,
    EmployeeName VARCHAR(50) NOT NULL,
    Phone        INT         NOT NULL,
    DepId        INT         NOT NULL
);
Rejects NULL on INSERT/UPDATE.
Ties into NULL rules: prefer NULL or empty-string policy — never blank spaces (see NULL notes).
4.2 DEFAULT — fallback value when none is supplied
CREATE TABLE Employees (
    EmployeeID   INT           PRIMARY KEY NOT NULL,
    EmployeeName VARCHAR(50)   NOT NULL,
    Phone        INT           NOT NULL,
    DepId        INT           NOT NULL,
    Salary       DECIMAL(10,2) DEFAULT 3000.00
);
Omit the column on INSERT → SQL fills in 3000.00 automatically.
4.3 PRIMARY KEY — uniquely identifies each row
Rules: unique + not null (one per table).

CREATE TABLE Employees (
    EmployeeID   INT           PRIMARY KEY NOT NULL,
    EmployeeName VARCHAR(50)   NOT NULL,
    Phone        INT           NOT NULL,
    DepId        INT           NOT NULL,
    Salary       DECIMAL(10,2) DEFAULT 3000.00
);
4.4 FOREIGN KEY — references a primary key in another table
The PK of one table gets inserted into another table as a link.

-- Parent table
CREATE TABLE tableDepartments (
    DepId          INT         PRIMARY KEY NOT NULL,
    DepartmentName VARCHAR(50) NOT NULL
);

-- Child table: DepId references the parent's PK
CREATE TABLE Employees (
    EmployeeID   INT           PRIMARY KEY NOT NULL,
    EmployeeName VARCHAR(50)   NOT NULL,
    Phone        INT           NOT NULL,
    DepId        INT           FOREIGN KEY REFERENCES tableDepartments(DepId) NOT NULL,
    Salary       DECIMAL(10,2) DEFAULT 3000.00
);
💡 FK guarantees referential integrity — you can't insert an employee into a department that doesn't exist.

4.5 UNIQUE — all values in a column must be different
CREATE TABLE Employees (
    EmployeeID   INT           PRIMARY KEY NOT NULL,
    EmployeeName VARCHAR(50)   NOT NULL,
    Phone        INT           UNIQUE,
    DepId        INT           FOREIGN KEY REFERENCES tableDepartments(DepId) NOT NULL,
    Salary       DECIMAL(10,2) DEFAULT 3000.00
);
Add UNIQUE later via a named constraint:

ALTER TABLE Employees
ADD CONSTRAINT U_phone UNIQUE (Phone);
4.6 CHECK (bonus) — values must satisfy a condition
CREATE TABLE Employees (
    EmployeeID   INT           PRIMARY KEY NOT NULL,
    EmployeeName VARCHAR(50)   NOT NULL,
    Phone        INT           NOT NULL,
    Age          INT           NOT NULL CHECK (Age >= 18),
    DepId        INT           NOT NULL
);
5. Full Example — Parent + Child Schema
CREATE DATABASE Company;
USE Company;

-- 1. Parent (departments)
CREATE TABLE tableDepartments (
    DepId          INT         PRIMARY KEY NOT NULL,
    DepartmentName VARCHAR(50) NOT NULL
);

-- 2. Child (employees) — uses every constraint at once
CREATE TABLE tableEmployees (
    EmployeeID   INT           PRIMARY KEY NOT NULL,          -- PK
    EmployeeName VARCHAR(50)   NOT NULL,                      -- NOT NULL
    Phone        INT           UNIQUE,                        -- UNIQUE
    Age          INT           NOT NULL CHECK (Age >= 18),    -- CHECK
    DepId        INT           NOT NULL,
    Salary       DECIMAL(10,2) DEFAULT 3000.00,               -- DEFAULT
    CONSTRAINT FK_emp_dept FOREIGN KEY (DepId)                -- FK
        REFERENCES tableDepartments(DepId)
);
📋 One-Glance Recap Table
Operation	Syntax	Effect
Create DB	CREATE DATABASE Company;	New empty database
Rename DB	ALTER DATABASE Test1 MODIFY NAME = 'Company';	Renames database
Select DB	USE Company;	Sets active database
Create table	CREATE TABLE T (col type constraints);	New empty table
Rename table	EXEC sp_rename 'Old', 'New';	Renames table
Drop table	DROP TABLE T;	Deletes table + data
Add constraint later	ALTER TABLE T ADD CONSTRAINT name UNIQUE(col);	Adds rule to existing table
Constraint	Guarantees	Rejects
NOT NULL	Column always has a value	NULL
DEFAULT	Fills value when none given	— (auto-fills)
PRIMARY KEY	Unique + not null row identity	Duplicates, NULLs
FOREIGN KEY	Value exists in parent table's PK	Orphan references
UNIQUE	No repeated values in column	Duplicates (NULL usually allowed)
CHECK	Condition is true (Age >= 18)	Failing values


⚡ Flashcards (test yourself)
Q: Primary Key rules? → A: Unique + NOT NULL, one per table.
Q: Which constraint auto-fills a value when you skip the column? → A: DEFAULT.
Q: FK references what in the other table? → A: Its Primary Key.
Q: sp_rename vs ALTER DATABASE ... MODIFY NAME? → A: Renames a table vs renames a database.
Q: CHAR(10) vs VARCHAR(10) for 'AB'? → A: CHAR pads to 10 chars; VARCHAR stores 2.
Q: Can UNIQUE columns hold NULL? → A: Usually yes (NULL ≠ duplicate) — PK columns cannot.
Q: Which constraint enforces Age >= 18? → A: CHECK.
Q: What does DROP TABLE remove? → A: Table structure AND all data — irreversible.
Q: Constraint that prevents inserting an employee into a nonexistent department? → A: FOREIGN KEY.
Q: Where must USE Company; run? → A: Before creating tables, so they land in the right DB.


✅ Practice Checklist
 Create a database, rename it, and USE it.
 Create a parent table with a PK and a child table with an FK referencing it.
 Try inserting a child row with a bad FK → observe the rejection.
 Insert without the Salary column → verify DEFAULT kicks in.
 Insert two rows with the same Phone → confirm UNIQUE blocks the second.
 Insert Age = 16 → confirm CHECK rejects it.
 Add a constraint after table creation with ALTER TABLE ... ADD CONSTRAINT.
 DROP the child table first, then the parent (FK order matters!).


